<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Flame: what a fire deals, and the one damage in this codebase that sets what it hits alight (system:
    fire). Its own file, not an addition to Damages.xml, for the usual reason (CLAUDE.md). Bound by
    SimWorld.Things.FireDamageDefOf.

    Distinct from the pre-existing Burn DamageDef, exactly as RimWorld ships both: Burn is plain heat damage
    with no ignition and stays untouched; Flame is fire's own, and adds DamageWorker_Flame on top. Both leave
    the same Burn injury and roll against the same Heat armor.

    canInterruptJobs false: a fire's damage never makes its victim re-ask the think tree mid-job
    (SimWorld.AI.Pawn_JobTracker.Notify_DamageTaken). A burning pawn is hit by the fire on its own back every
    complex-calc pass; if each hit re-decided, every run leg of the burning tier would be cut short and the
    self-extinguish roll re-rolled while it was being carried out. Being set alight still ends whatever the
    pawn was doing, through its own path (FireUtility.TryAttachFire). The field is RimWorld's
    (Verse.DamageDef.canInterruptJobs); RimWorld's value for Flame is in its Def XML, which the decompile does
    not carry, so it is pinned by BurningResponseTests rather than claimed as sourced.
  -->

  <DamageDef>
    <defName>Flame</defName>
    <label>flame</label>
    <workerClass>SimWorld.Health.DamageWorker_Flame</workerClass>
    <hediff>Burn</hediff>
    <makesBlood>false</makesBlood>
    <hasForcefulImpact>false</hasForcefulImpact>
    <armorCategory>Heat</armorCategory>
    <deathMessage>{0} has burned to death.</deathMessage>
    <canInterruptJobs>false</canInterruptJobs>
  </DamageDef>

</Defs>
